---
import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/latin-400.css";
import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/latin-500.css";
import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/latin-600.css";
import SiteLayout from "@/layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { getPublishedEntries } from "@/lib/content";
import { url } from "@/lib/paths";

const entries = await getPublishedEntries();
const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "voice-and-rhythm", text: "Voice and rhythm" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "reading-at-length", text: "Reading at length" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "technical-language", text: "Technical language" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "code-and-detail", text: "Code and detail" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "compare-the-typefaces", text: "Compare the typefaces" },
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="IBM Plex Mono Type Specimen — Zephyr"
  description="A long-form specimen for IBM Plex Mono, including display sizes, reading text, lists, quotations, and code."
  entries={entries}
  headings={headings}
  noindex
>
  <div class="type-specimen article-prose article-prose--ibm-plex-mono" data-article-body>
    <header class="type-specimen__hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">TYPE SPECIMEN / MONOSPACE</p>
      <h1 class="type-specimen__title">IBM Plex<br />Mono</h1>
      <p class="type-specimen__lede">
        A monospaced typeface with a measured, technical voice. This page uses IBM Plex Mono
        for every visible heading, paragraph, label, quotation, and code sample so the
        rhythm can be judged in a real reading layout.
      </p>
      <p class="type-specimen__glyphs" aria-label="IBM Plex Mono glyph sample">
        ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ<br />
        abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz<br />
        0123456789 &amp; @ # % ? ! / \ ( ) [ ] { }
      </p>
      <div class="type-specimen__meta">
        <span>IBM Plex Mono</span>
        <span>400 / 500 / 600</span>
        <span>Latin subset</span>
        <span>Self-hosted</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2 id="voice-and-rhythm">A monospaced voice with a human edge</h2>
      <p>
        IBM Plex Mono belongs to the wider IBM Plex family, a system designed to connect
        brand, engineering, and editorial communication. Its monospaced construction gives
        every glyph the same horizontal advance, so letters, numbers, punctuation, and code
        align into a steady grid. That mechanical consistency is useful for scanning
        technical information, but it does not make the typeface cold. The curves remain
        open, the terminals have subtle modulation, and the overall texture feels more
        written than engineered.
      </p>
      <p>
        The result is a typeface that can move between documentation, interface labels,
        code, and long-form explanation without changing its personality. A proportional
        sans-serif compresses and expands according to the shape of each letter. A
        monospaced face keeps the same beat. IBM Plex Mono makes that beat visible enough
        to establish a technical atmosphere while leaving enough softness for sustained
        reading.
      </p>
      <p>
        This specimen is intentionally dense. Short samples reveal the outline of a
        typeface; longer passages reveal its rhythm. Reading several paragraphs at once
        shows how the eye moves across repeated widths, how much white space accumulates
        between words, and whether the line endings create a calm texture or a rigid wall
        of characters.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="reading-at-length">Reading at length</h2>
      <p>
        A monospaced book face is uncommon for a reason. Equal widths remove the natural
        compression of letters such as i, l, and t, while giving wide characters such as m
        and W the same footprint. That tradeoff creates clarity and consistency, but it
        also increases the physical width of every line. A paragraph in IBM Plex Mono
        occupies more horizontal space than the same paragraph in Manrope, which means
        fewer words fit inside a fixed column and the reader encounters more frequent line
        breaks.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those extra line breaks can be beneficial. When the text describes code,
        architecture, interfaces, versions, commands, measurements, or systems, the
        regularity reinforces the subject. The page begins to resemble a carefully
        maintained technical document. The eye can jump from one line to the next without
        being distracted by changing letter widths. The tradeoff is speed. Long prose in a
        monospaced face asks for a slower reading pace, and that pace can be exactly what a
        technical article needs.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          The typeface does not merely carry the content. Its rhythm tells the reader what
          kind of content to expect: deliberate, structured, inspectable, and precise.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        At this width, IBM Plex Mono reads as a text face rather than a terminal font. The
        lowercase forms are generous, the punctuation remains visible, and the capitals do
        not overwhelm the line. The italic style is available in the full family, although
        this specimen keeps the primary reading surface upright so that weight and spacing
        can be compared without additional variation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="technical-language">Technical language and interface detail</h2>
      <p>
        The typeface becomes especially convincing when it handles mixed content. A line
        can move from ordinary explanation into a command, a version number, a file path,
        or an API name without changing its fundamental texture. This continuity is useful
        in documentation because the reader does not have to treat code as a separate
        visual world. Prose and technical notation share the same grid while still being
        distinguished by weight, surface, and spacing.
      </p>
      <h3>Clear structure</h3>
      <p>
        Headings in IBM Plex Mono feel like chapter markers in a technical manual. The
        letters are evenly spaced, the silhouette is compact, and the line can carry a
        strong label without requiring excessive weight. A medium weight establishes
        hierarchy while retaining the precision of the regular face.
      </p>
      <h3>Deliberate spacing</h3>
      <p>
        Monospaced text benefits from generous line height. The fixed width already creates
        strong horizontal rhythm, so the vertical interval should give each line enough air
        to prevent the paragraph from becoming a dense block. This page keeps the site's
        established reading size and line height, allowing the typeface itself to reveal how
        much additional width it introduces.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Regular weight suits long explanations, notes, and reference material.</li>
        <li>Medium weight works well for headings, labels, navigation, and emphasis.</li>
        <li>The fixed grid supports code, numeric tables, paths, and repeated structures.</li>
        <li>Open counters keep small text legible without making the face look playful.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="code-and-detail">Code and detail</h2>
      <p>
        A monospaced face is most familiar in code, and IBM Plex Mono carries that
        association without reducing the surrounding page to a terminal. The sample below
        shows how the same family can preserve alignment, distinguish punctuation, and
        remain comfortable beside regular prose.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`const specimen = {
  family: "IBM Plex Mono",
  spacing: "monospaced",
  weights: [400, 500, 600],
  purpose: "technical reading",
};

function renderTypeface(font) {
  return font
    .split(" ")
    .map((word) => word.toUpperCase())
    .join(" / ");
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Compare the block above with the adjacent paragraphs. The code sample is not
        switching to a different family; it inherits the same IBM Plex Mono voice. What
        changes is the density of the line and the visual purpose of the content. The code
        grid and the prose grid belong to one system.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="compare-the-typefaces">Compare the typefaces</h2>
      <p>
        The most useful comparison is not a single word set at a large size. Read the same
        kind of material across all three pages and observe line endings, word spacing, and
        how quickly the eye returns to the start of the next line.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href={url("/weekly/2026-w44/")}>Nov 1: Manrope</a> shows a proportional, compact reading rhythm.</li>
        <li><a href={url("/weekly/2026-w43/")}>Oct 25: JetBrains Mono</a> shows a sharper coding-oriented monospace texture.</li>
        <li>This page: IBM Plex Mono shows a softer, editorial approach to monospaced reading.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Manrope changes width from letter to letter and produces the most conventional prose
        rhythm. JetBrains Mono emphasizes technical precision, squared forms, and unmistakable
        character separation. IBM Plex Mono sits between those poles: it keeps the fixed grid
        of code while preserving a human, publication-oriented finish.
      </p>
      <p>
        The difference becomes clear at paragraph scale. IBM Plex Mono is wider and more
        regular than Manrope, but rounder and more editorial than JetBrains Mono. It is a
        strong option when the site should feel technical without looking like an IDE.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
